{"reply":"In Chapter 5 of *Animal Farm*, Squealer says:

> “Do not imagine, comrades, that leadership is a pleasure! On the contrary, it is a deep and heavy responsibility.”

The wording presents leadership as sacrifice, but in the surrounding argument that claimed burden becomes a reason the animals should no longer make decisions for themselves. The loss of their say is then framed as protection from “wrong decisions,” followed by the end of the Sunday debates. The parallel with your project lead is worth examining, but it does not prove their motive: in both cases, “I’m carrying the burden” can function either as context for a shared decision or as a reason others should stop questioning. Your guilt may be worth separating from the practical question of whether ending the planning meetings left the team without a meaningful voice. Does your guilt come from something specific you did, or mainly from the suggestion that questioning the reversal means adding to the burden?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pga0100011-vc7ff4da7-ch05-ln1133-1158","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Chapter V, source lines 1133-1158","exact_quote":"“Do not imagine, comrades, that leadership is a pleasure! On the contrary, it is a deep and heavy responsibility.”","supported_claims":["In Chapter 5 of *Animal Farm*, Squealer says:","The wording presents leadership as sacrifice, but in the surrounding argument that claimed burden becomes a reason the animals should no longer make decisions for themselves.","The loss of their say is then framed as protection from “wrong decisions,” followed by the end of the Sunday debates."]}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
